#{[17592186130151 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1061 36261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ae24fd7-3348-5c2c-97a8-bf4a352b05a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130151 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1061 36263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e1aa31d-da89-5630-8461-cb2becfe2266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130152 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1061 36266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1b0c4be-3561-5047-b474-fc789baf401e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130153 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1061 36625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "120df569-6a65-5f8d-9c7d-5fcfadd9b271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130161 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1061 36626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d13633da-c1a6-540e-a940-eff75190b892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130162 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1064 "When adding numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the sum is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational. Rational numbers can be represented as fractions of the form $\\dfrac ab$ (where $a$ and $b$ are integers), and adding two of these fractions always produces another fraction.\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\dfrac12$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the sum in decimal form, we get\n$1.414213562… + 0.5 = 1.914213562…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following sum is irrational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt 3 = 3.146\\,264\\,37\\ldots$\nHowever, the following sum is rational:\n$\\sqrt 2 + (-\\sqrt 2) = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1065 5941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1b2b00f9-12fd-5755-9340-1a9477f61cdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130432 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1064 "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3}; = \\sqrt{6}$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 2}; = \\sqrt{2^{2}}; = 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1065 5942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74425e69-1e7a-5c1c-b1ac-2aec2e2ad1df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130433 #edb/ref 1067 "Products of Rational and Irrational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1064 "To summarize:\n\n- the sum (or difference) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} + \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the sum (or difference) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1065 5998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "df9f71cf-81c8-546c-99f3-cca45192f745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130437 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1064 "To summarize:\n\n- the product (or quotient) of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{rational} = \\textrm{rational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n$\\textrm{rational} \\times \\textrm{irrational} = \\textrm{irrational}$\n- the product (or quotient) of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nFinally, it's worth pointing out that if $a$ is irrational, then the second rule implies that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1a\n$$\n\nis also irrational." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1065 5999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "45f6a1bf-6d91-5130-8aa5-a2d5863e2654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130438 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133976 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "811ba45c-7467-5cef-9fb8-a8282891b607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Polygons That Map to Each Other Under Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133977 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cfe2598e-57bd-53fb-8477-9c4c5a8d3fa4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Why Polygons Are Congruent Using Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133978 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e4ffa315-2f50-51da-a928-329e1816706f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Maps Presented in Function Notation That Are Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133979 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b01281fd-5272-579c-98d5-ca5cc00c73e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186135010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1045 "Rigid Motions and Congruence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186135006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134053 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e6580470-6b81-57e7-bf68-123e2ad0a485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1061 37342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6caa4091-01f6-57a9-8303-9c7ac2687088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1061 37367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b767960-73f6-5f1a-86d5-06e3a151b4d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1061 9555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66e47972-9ac9-5e15-abc6-6b2f033b161a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135007 #edb/ref 1061 9556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43999184-e956-5a86-8726-8697e9473d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1061 9558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21aa14a0-3abb-5d4a-92b6-d08d24876eb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186135010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1061 9562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "971e2526-fd20-58e6-bc97-09ccc8b2617e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1061 9567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a91d4728-a024-5878-9599-76838230c6ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1064 "A **rigid motion** is any transformation that does not change the distances and angles between points. Rigid motions are also called **isometries**.\n\nThere are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\n**Watch out!** The following types of transformations are *not* rigid motions:\n\n- Dilations\n- Stretches" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1065 2294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9208af14-0cfd-5ca9-989f-3bf18330cf12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135021 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1064 "As we mentioned earlier, rigid motions are **distance-preserving**.\n\nFor example, if a rigid motion maps $\\triangle ABC$ to $\\triangle A'B'C'$, then the corresponding sides of the triangles must be congruent:\n\n$$\nAB = A'B', \\qquad AC = A'C', \\qquad BC = B'C'\n$$\n\nIn addition, rigid motions are **angle-preserving**, which means that the corresponding angles of the triangles must be congruent as well:\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = m\\angle A', \\qquad m\\angle B = m\\angle B', \\qquad m\\angle C = m\\angle C'\n$$\n\nSince all of the corresponding sides and angles are congruent, the triangles are congruent, and we write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle A'B'C'\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1065 6157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4514dc0f-bb78-52d9-a728-8a09f7cbf667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135147 #edb/ref 1067 "Further Properties of Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1064 "So, remember, there are *only three* types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\nThere are two properties of rigid motions we need to know:\n\n- *A rigid motion maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.*\n- *For any pair of congruent polygons, there exists a rigid motion that maps one polygon onto another.*\n\nConsider the diagram below. Let's use the concept of rigid motions to explain why $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ are congruent.\n\n![](images/04/0487c9bb2a640a44e9ee67830937db4272c97f4a5f5101e43e3dcf3edd6c3a1c.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathcal{Q}$ is the image of $\\mathcal{P}$ under the action of reflection in the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/18/183b182e26df36c6cfe6a9d682e0f3b000bff40ce7d405255a0abfee8529a946.png)\n\nSince $\\mathcal P$ is mapped to $\\mathcal Q$ under a rigid motion (reflection), $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ must be congruent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1065 6163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "511ff35e-2666-5989-ad2f-e3e9fe541c7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135148 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Rigid Motions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Spheres, Hemispheres, Cones, and Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147455 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f4196423-3401-54e1-b214-9dbc4861cfe1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147461 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147462 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b608