2880 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172880 #edb/ref 1061 30461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172880 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a817a13-cf12-5a56-abd1-7fbe139fe7b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172880 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172881 #edb/ref 1061 30463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172881 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b6bd47e-dc8a-5923-bbcc-1f0390d2d1db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172881 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1061 32450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "213cee54-dfad-5696-a210-97d588e61069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173097 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173097 #edb/ref 1061 458344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173097 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19378221-e834-5c4b-990e-07c0375a2e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173097 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173098 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173098 #edb/ref 1061 458345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173098 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51e398bc-e128-5132-8544-f247f071eb64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173098 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173099 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173099 #edb/ref 1061 458349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173099 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c8b71e0-5a17-55a7-a089-43d023c422de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173099 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173100 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173100 #edb/ref 1061 458352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173100 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cf650db5-7716-5a21-8630-34c4a846767d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173100 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173101 #edb/ref 1061 458355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ee3f8d6-f452-5eee-97bb-9be3735604ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1064 "When finding the roots of a transformed radical function, we need to consider whether the radical's index is odd or even.\n\nLet's start with the odd case first. As an example, we will find the root of the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[{\\color{blue}{3}}]{10-4x} + 2\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that we're dealing with a cube root, and so the index of the radical is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, which is odd.\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} &= -2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x})^{3} &= (-2)^{3} \\\\\n10 - 4x &= -8 \\\\\n- 4x &= -18 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac92\n$$\n\nA transformed radical function with an odd index will always have one real root." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1065 4864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a49268e2-e58c-5966-ba98-b1786827ba76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now learn how to find the root of a radical function with an even index. For example, let's try to find the root of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9\n$$\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= -9 \\\\\n\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1})^{4} &= 3^{4} \\\\\n10x + 1 &= 81 \\\\\n10x &= 80 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10 \\cdot 8 + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{81} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3 \\cdot 3 + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8$.\n\nWhen the index of the radical function is even, we should always check for extraneous roots by substituting them back into the original equation.\n\nSometimes, a radical function with an even index has no root. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1065 5161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a1feb60-62da-5279-8bfd-f8be6c389de7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function: Even Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173174 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the parabola's vertex.\n\nIf we are given the equation of a parabola in standard form, we can obtain its vertex form by completing the square.\n\nFor example, consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny= x^2+6x+16\n$$\n\nTo express the equation of this parabola in vertex form, we complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + 6x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 9 + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x - (-3))^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex of the parabola is at the point $({\\color{blue}-3},{\\color{red}7})$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173174 #edb/ref 1065 5186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173174 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "98a78c68-d6fd-5642-a60b-1ac9e65d17ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173174 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173281 #edb/ref 1064 "**Note**: You may find the algebra in this section a little tricky. It's a good idea to go through it, but you will not be quizzed on it. You may skip this section if you wish.\n\n![](images/2a/2a400b12675f8ebf19ba563192d9ae2296336e40ea380d40e6247564718a2b1f.png)\n\nYou may have seen already that for the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe **axis of symmetry** of this parabola is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to derive this formula.\n\nSince\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can take out a factor of $a$ from the right-hand side in the parabola formula:\n\n$$\ny = a\\left(x^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + \\dfrac{c}{a} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2}_{\\textrm{perfect square}} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2} = \\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 + \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na((x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}}) &= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + a \\cdot \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}} \\\\\n&= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a} \\\\\n&= a(x - (-\\frac{b}{2a})_(⏟)_(h))^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a}_(⏟)_(k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy denoting\n\n$$\nh=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\qquad k=\\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation of the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere the vertex is given by\n\n$$\n(h,k) = \\left(-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since the parabola's axis of symmetry is the vertical line passing through the vertex, the equation of the axis must be\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173281 #edb/ref 1065 9116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173281 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41e62e06-e589-55dc-846e-1177555ce582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173281 #edb/ref 1067 "Derivation of the Axis of Symmetry Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176033 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176033 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176033 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176033 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Residuals From a Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176033 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176033 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176034 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176034 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176034 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176034 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176034 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176034 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176035 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176035 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176035 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176035 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176035 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176035 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aa97e57a-70d8-5708-8b7d-fa05604a3f88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1045 "Residuals and Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176138 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8f647d7f-d10f-52b0-a5c4-c95324d956b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1061 36423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56d03218-9fd1-5cb5-a471-09641e54c0ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176826 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1061 36424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5ff3834-5f43-5149-a054-8bfcbb8b3bbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176827 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1061 36425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2dbc11bb-69e2-5610-9c8e-28e53d57e103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176828 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1061 36426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e65742ab-c850-50fd-b1a4-0670ddc16c2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176829 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176830 #edb/ref 1061 36427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176830 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7847754-3ac7-5ffa-af5a-838c5d3e8886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176830 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1061 36805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3fdb60d4-7e70-551a-9e4a-8bb61625c9cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176833 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model\n\n$$\n\\widehat{y} =f(x)\n$$\n\nthe **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - f(x) \\\\\n&= y - y^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value.\n\nTo illustrate this concept graphically, consider the following example.\n\nThe owner of an ice cream shop keeps track of the average daily temperature $x$ (measured in $^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$) and the resulting daily ice cream sales $y$ (in dollars). The scatter plot for several randomly selected days and the corresponding linear regression line are shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc149b8d911a7a9863df9cc25c635d6155accd676e83e335a65d9083596b779b.png)\n\nSuppose the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny= 2.5 x +165\n$$\n\nLet's use our regression line to compute the residual of the point with coordinates $\\color{red}(82, 360)$ (shown in red on our graph).\n\nFirst, notice that since the highlighted data point has coordinates ${\\color{red}(82, 360)}$, our actual value is\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{red}360}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^(^) &= 2.5x + 165 \\\\\n&= 2.5(82) + 165 \\\\\n&= 370\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/38/3843c71b50251ae035378507ffb22cfade48dff8e1544d8e1317e4e6d611f7a1.png)\n\nFinally, we compute the residual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - y^(^) \\\\\n&= 360 - 370 \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residual tells us that the linear model *overestimates* the true volume of ice cream sales by $\\[math]10$ when the outside temperature is $82^\\circ\\,\\textrm{F}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1065 5968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7e5cd2f9-494e-5533-98e9-c24f42631dad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177047 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1064 "A **residual plot** displays the values of the independent variable along the horizontal axis and the residuals along the vertical axis.\n\nThe scatter plot below represents data from a four-day sample on the number of people visiting a swimming pool and the average daytime temperature, in degrees Celsius. The line $\\ell$ is the corresponding linear regression line.\n\n![](images/62/6228578012fac7e42bedeb7ab7ad5fc9f42432aed8d5751b675df560bc867417.png)\n\nThese residuals for each of the four data points are highlighted below:\n\n![](images/d7/d70c44fe7b8fce43a8962ac104651acced75e10598e865a3b3c80d09f16ce234.png)\n\nIf we calculate the numerical values for the residuals at each data point (from left to right), we get the following results:\n\n$$\n-4, \\quad 8, \\quad -8, \\quad 4\n$$\n\nTo compute our residual plot, we plot each $x$-value and its corresponding residual on some coordinate axes:\n\n![](images/ff/ff8e6da5489313f1f981c379505f9a72610dfd2a4b5dc0113685d647ba02a1f3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1065 5969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddf8fe09-fc0b-546d-942f-17a086708a11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177048 #edb/ref 1067 "Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/ff/ff8e6da5489313f1f981c379505f9a72610dfd2a4b5dc0113685d647ba02a1f3.png)\n\nWe should be aware of the following interpretations for residual plots:\n\n- Any point *above* the horizontal axis represents a *positive* residual. A positive residual means that the regression model *underestimates* the observed value for $y$ at this point.\n- Any point *below* the horizontal axis represents a *negative* residual. A negative residual means that the regression model *overestimates* the observed value for $y$ at this point.\n\nResidual plots can also be used to assess whether a trend line is a \"good\" fit for the corresponding data:\n\n- If a linear trend line is a good fit for the data, the residuals should be scattered randomly above and below the horizontal axis and be relatively close to zero.\n- In cases where the residuals show some clear pattern, this usually indicates that the data has no linear trend.\n\nThe residuals are scattered randomly about the horizontal axis in our last example. However, they are not close to zero, indicating that the trend line is a weak fit for the data." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1065 6034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d7794124-93e8-5abb-8a78-4c8884a07547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177051 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Residual Plots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1039 "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179648 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1039 "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179649 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3db20b82-9561-5c54-8e12-8914bb6747c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1045 "Adding Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179887 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "83044b10-d4a9-5617-a56e-6d6330eeb3ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1061 18875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e650778-2844-52ef-8b57-d9a72869e882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1061 18876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "619ce5a9-f7df-5e65-b3c8-68102bc19437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180352 #edb/ref 1061 18879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "833d312d-0148-58a3-bccf-4edf9a9c2020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nwe need to put the fractions over a **common denominator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt works the same in algebra! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1065 3377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f5b564ba-0934-5110-87ce-f04259821232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184193 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184193 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Shift Only Inside Parentheses" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184193 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184193 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0aa53b10-4f0e-59b6-b00a-f5bd07a34b14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184227 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184227 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184227 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184227 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Stretch Followed By Horizontal Shift Inside Parentheses" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184227 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184227 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c76594da-4831-56c4-8c49-68349ba62a19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184234 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184234 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184234 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Stretch Only Inside Parentheses" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184234 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186183916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184234 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7797323b-5c5c-55db-b2d9-4184556dc18a" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
